WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.171 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One team wins eight games out of ten, and another wins
seventy-five out of one hundred.

00:00:05.391 --> 00:00:08.426 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which record is the greater relative success?

00:00:09.076 --> 00:00:15.989 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are comparing shares fairly by using a common whole
and connecting fractions, decimals and percentages.

00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:23.329 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to use decimal and percentage
representations to compare real-world relative amounts.

00:00:23.979 --> 00:00:25.736 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:25.956 --> 00:00:30.113 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can convert familiar decimals and fractions into
percentages.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:34.529 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can compare amounts using a common whole or a scale of
100.

00:00:34.749 --> 00:00:39.113 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can explain why comparing numbers in different forms can
be misleading.

00:00:39.763 --> 00:00:43.100 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Counts alone can be misleading when the totals differ.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.230 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare the share of each total by renaming it on a common
scale.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:52.720 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Percentages use a scale of one hundred; they do not tell us
how many items there were originally.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.935 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Team A wins eight out of ten games.

00:00:56.155 --> 00:01:00.381 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Rename the share as eighty out of one hundred, which is
eighty percent.

00:01:01.031 --> 00:01:06.257 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Team B wins seventy-five out of one hundred games, which is
seventy-five percent.

00:01:06.477 --> 00:01:12.915 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eighty percent is greater, so Team A has the greater
relative success, although Team B has more wins in total.

00:01:13.565 --> 00:01:18.290 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare a bar that is half shaded with a bar that is zero
point four shaded.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.272 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use equal complete bars for the wholes.

00:01:21.922 --> 00:01:25.888 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Half is fifty percent and zero point four is forty percent.

00:01:26.108 --> 00:01:30.809 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Half is the greater share because fifty hundredths is more
than forty hundredths.

00:01:31.459 --> 00:01:35.080 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which share is greater: three quarters or seventy percent?

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.862 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three quarters is seventy-five percent.

00:01:42.082 --> 00:01:46.049 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That is greater than seventy percent by five percentage
points.

00:01:46.699 --> 00:01:51.830 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Identify each whole, rename shares on a common scale, and
compare relative size.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:56.830 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A greater percentage does not necessarily mean a greater
count when the totals differ.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.266 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain which comparison the question asks for.
